<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        CustomField.description is capped at 255 characters; this rationale is longer and
        is operationally important. The comment is INSIDE the root element on purpose - a
        comment ABOVE <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at conversion.

        THE DENOMINATOR IS NINE, IT IS A NAMED CONSTANT LIST, AND IT IS SIGNED OFF.
        Signed off 2026-08-14 (spec sec 0.0, requirements O4). The nine DEAL-PROCESS keys:

            property_name          property_address       guidance_price
            guidance_cap_rate      offer_due_date         sale_process
            listing_broker_name    listing_broker_email   listing_status

        The six PHYSICAL-PROPERTY keys - building_sf, noi, unit_count, occupancy,
        year_built, asset_type - are EXCLUDED. A land deal legitimately has no building SF
        and no NOI; scoring it against them would report a complete extraction as a poor
        one, and the score's whole purpose is to tell an analyst which leads need chasing.

        DO NOT DERIVE THE DENOMINATOR FROM A DESCRIBE. Spec sec 2.2 is explicit: a
        describe-based denominator silently changes the meaning of every score in the org
        the moment a field is added. The list is a constant in Apex and the ninth key
        (listing_status) is new in this same change.

        NOT Parse_Confidence__c. THE TWO MUST NEVER BE CONFLATED, AND THE FAILURE MODE IS
        SUBTLE. Parse_Confidence__c records the model's certainty about ONE question -
        is_acquisition_related - which is what the D2 hard relevance gate consumes. A
        confident, correctly-classified acquisition email can still be almost empty of
        deal facts, and an unconfident one can be complete. HIGH confidence therefore does
        NOT imply a high score and a low score does NOT impeach the classification. Both
        fields are retained, unchanged, side by side.

        FAIL-SOFT IS MANDATORY, NOT DEFENSIVE POLISH. Spec sec 1 constraint 7: the inbound
        email pipeline must never be made to throw. A defect in scoring must cost nothing
        but the score - a NULL here plus a Deal_Notes__c note, never an exception. The
        precedent is InboundEmailFieldUtil, which clips every externally sourced value for
        exactly this reason. NULL is therefore a MEANINGFUL VALUE here: it reads "not
        scored", which is not the same as zero.

        PRECISION 3 / SCALE 0 is the whole domain - 0 to 100 inclusive. It is not a
        constraint being applied; it is the range the value can occupy.
    -->
    <fullName>Extraction_Score_Pct__c</fullName>
    <description>Percent of the nine signed-off deal-process extraction keys captured from the broker email. NOT Parse_Confidence__c, which measures certainty about is_acquisition_related - a different question. Null means not scored, which is not zero.</description>
    <externalId>false</externalId>
    <inlineHelpText>How complete the automated extraction was, 0-100. A low score means chase the broker for the missing deal facts. Blank means the score could not be computed, not that nothing was captured.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Extraction Score %</label>
    <precision>3</precision>
    <required>false</required>
    <scale>0</scale>
    <trackFeedHistory>false</trackFeedHistory>
    <type>Number</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
